I just finished talking to myself for about the past hour, a couple of hours, and I've really been reflecting on my place in the world and where I am. There's this painting by Ivan Aivazovsky, a marine artist who paints these amazing, realistic paintings. I don't really like the sad and worrying ones, but the one that really impacted me is The Ninth Wave (1850), one of his most famous pieces, and I really see why. It's a painting of the sun, and you see survivors of a wreck clinging to the wreckage, and it resembles a cross a little bit. They're basically cooked, but they're also reaching out for something, reaching out for the future, and the sun is there like God is speaking to them. It's just beautiful.

I feel like that's who I am. Even though I'm fucked, even though I'm in the middle of nowhere in the ocean, in the worst situation possible, I'm unrealistically optimistic. I'm so optimistic that I turn a bad situation into a good one. And the waves are beautiful. The way he painted the light reflecting off them just sparks something in me. Maybe I'm stretching it because I feel so good about this painting, but it's like hope is refracting off despair.

What it reflects in my life is that I wanted to be an engineer. I thought I was cooked. It's way past my time to be an engineer, way past my time for all these dreams and hopes I had of being part of something huge, like the Mars rover Perseverance. It's already sunk. But there's a new day rising, and I still feel hopeful. It sounds weird, but it's my enjoyment of tech, coding, technology in general, and I'm hoping to rediscover that. I think that's the point of this painting for me.

It also resonates because I've been struggling with faith, struggling with God, personally. But I still see that light, and I still have faith. I still have love and joy. I love looking into the sun, seeing the bigger picture and the waves move. Maybe I like this ocean picture, the sun, all of it, because I feel like I'm part of something bigger than myself. It sparks something in me, and I just don't know how to explain it.